      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to (-5/2)}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274353/assets/f43a774fdfe638719972b74163adc61dc1099ab0117bda1c5bcc648912fd7b25.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le -\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -5/{2}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>-\\frac{5}{2}.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -5/{2}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are not equal, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -5/2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+5)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$",
